Composery sign-in, end to end.

Password lifecycle: first boot has no password - register (or the cloud
authorize flow) creates it, change-password changes it, and the server treats
the no-password state as unauthenticated instead of refusing to start. login
renders through the shared auth shell (renderAuthPage) with redirect-style
errors; env passwords stay mutually exclusive; the generated-password default
config is gone (register owns creation). Deletes upstream's standalone
login/error pages - the overlay auth shell replaces them (error.html and
global.css are re-provided by the overlay).

Checking a password collapses to one overlay function. Upstream carried a
PasswordMethod dispatch (legacy SHA-256, a cookie-validation twin, and an
argon2 hash computed on every attempt whose only consumer was the cookie this
stack no longer sets) for a product with no legacy passwords to support. What
survives is `isPasswordValid(args, password)`. Because argon2 is now the only
format, a hashed-password in any other shape stops the IDE at start: nothing
could ever match it, so accepting it means looking configured while refusing
every sign-in.

/login and the current-password step of /change-password answer the same
question, so they share one per-source budget rather than each holding a
limiter that the other is a way around.

Workbench commands: Sign Out and Change Password are registered when the
matching endpoint is in product.json, both routed through openAuthEndpoint,
which suppresses beforeunload for the deliberate navigation. The endpoints
resolve against window.top so they work embedded. Whether change-password
exists at all is one predicate (isEnvPasswordManaged) shared by the command,
the route guard and the link on the sign-in page - a cloud instance's own
COMPOSERY_HASHED_PASSWORD is not "the environment owns this", because the
change is recorded with the website first and reconciled back into it.

COMPOSERY_DISABLE_AUTH turns sign-in off for deployments with their own gate
in front (a private network, an SSO proxy, a throwaway local instance). Upstream
carries AuthType.None end to end - authenticated() short-circuits, so the
workbench, both proxies and every websocket open together, and the sign-in
commands drop out with the endpoints behind them. Only an explicit 1/true
disables: every other value, typos included, fails towards keeping sign-in.
The boot line becomes a warning that names the real exposure - a cloud instance
sits on the public internet where a self-hosted one may be loopback-only -
and a configured-but-ignored password says so.

